tel 1.0

base prescience
import soundness

info   a proof of concept: typeclass instances that generate code at macro expansion, evaluated live by the deriving macro
detail
    _Prescience_ demonstrates one technique: a typeclass, `Inlinable`, whose primary method is a
    code generator — it takes an `Expr` of its input and returns an `Expr` of the decoded value —
    so that a deriving macro can splice a hand-written instance's code directly into the code it
    generates, rather than a call to the instance at runtime. It is the mechanism behind the
    `Inlinable` typeclasses of the format libraries' staged readers and writers (`jacinta`,
    `breviloquence`, `locomotion`, `stratiform`'s BinTEL), proved here in isolation over a toy
    wire format.

    The difficulty is that a macro holds only an `Expr` of an instance it summons, while it needs
    the live instance to call `read` on. The deriving macro, `Prescience.read`, obtains it in up
    to three tiers, per field:

    - **Tier A**: an instance which is a reference to a precompiled static object, or a
      parameterless member of one, is loaded through the macro classloader and used directly,
      as the compiler's own splice interpreter loads a macro implementation.
    - **Tier B** (`Prescience.readStaging` only): an instance which implicit search can find
      from global scope (companions and package-level givens on the classpath), including a
      conditional given, is summoned inside a fresh in-macro `staging.Compiler` and run, at the
      cost of a full compiler run per instance, reported as an info diagnostic.
    - **Runtime**: anything else — a lexically scoped given, or one compiled in the same run as
      the use site — gets a spliced call to its `Inlinable#readRuntime` instead.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import prescience.*
    import prescience.inlinables.{int, string, boolean}

    case class Order(id: Int, name: String, active: Boolean)

    Prescience.read[Order]("7,widget,true")   // Order(7, "widget", true)
    ```

    An instance defined in the compilation run that expands the macro is never evaluated,
    in either tier: its classfile either does not exist yet or is stale from a previous
    incremental compile, so it always takes the runtime tier, and clean and incremental builds
    agree. The output is the same in every tier; only where the code was generated differs.

    The library has two source sets, both JVM-only, since the technique needs the macro
    classloader and the `scala3-compiler` and `scala3-staging` artifacts: `prescience.core`
    (`Inlinable`, `Prescience` and the macro) and `prescience.leaves` (sample instances,
    compiled one stage earlier than any use site, so that tier A can load them). Nothing is
    exported to the `soundness` umbrella: import `prescience.*`, and the sample givens by name
    from `prescience.inlinables`, since `import pkg.*` does not import givens.

refer prescience.Inlinable
refer prescience.Prescience
refer prescience.inlinables

entry .Inlinable   a typeclass whose instances generate the code which reads their type, for a deriving macro to inline
  detail
      `T is Inlinable` has two methods which must agree: `#read`, which is run at macro expansion
      and returns the *code* reading a `T` from a `String` expression, and `#readRuntime`, the
      same reading done at runtime, which the deriving macro calls when it cannot obtain the
      live instance at expansion time.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import prescience.*
      import scala.quoted.*

      case class Rank(value: Int)

      object Rank:
        given inlinable: (Rank is Inlinable) = new Inlinable:
          type Self = Rank

          def read(input: Expr[String])(using Quotes): Expr[Rank] =
            '{Rank(java.lang.Integer.parseInt($input.trim.nn))}

          def readRuntime(input: String): Rank =
            Rank(java.lang.Integer.parseInt(input.trim.nn))
      ```

      Whether `#read` is used depends on where the instance is: a static object or a member of
      one, compiled earlier than the use site, is evaluated by `Prescience.read`; a conditional
      given in a companion only by `Prescience.readStaging`; anything compiled in the same run,
      or scoped lexically, only ever through `#readRuntime`.
  refer prescience.Prescience
  entry #read   the code reading a value of this type from a `String` expression, generated at macro expansion
      Called by the deriving macro with an `Expr[String]` of the field's text and a `Quotes`; the
      returned `Expr[Self]` is spliced into the generated reader in place of any call.
  entry #readRuntime   reads a value of this type from text at runtime, when the instance cannot be evaluated at expansion
      Must give the same result as the code `#read` generates, since which of the two is used
      depends on where the instance is defined and how the program is being compiled.

entry .Prescience   the deriving macros: a case-class reader assembled from its fields' `Inlinable` instances
  detail
      Each method derives, for a non-generic case class, a reader from a line of comma-separated
      fields (a toy format: the mechanism is the point): the input is split once, and each
      field's code comes from its type's `Inlinable` instance, inlined when the instance can be
      evaluated at expansion time and a call to `Inlinable#readRuntime` otherwise.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import prescience.*
      import prescience.inlinables.int

      case class Point(x: Int, y: Int)

      Prescience.read[Point]("3,4")   // Point(3, 4)
      ```

      A type which is not a case class, a generic case class, or a field type with no
      `Inlinable` in scope, is a compile error.
  refer prescience.Inlinable
  entry .read   reads a case class from comma-separated text, inlining every statically evaluable field instance
      Tier A only: an instance which resolves to a precompiled static object, or a
      parameterless member of one, contributes its `Inlinable#read` code; any other instance is
      called at runtime through `Inlinable#readRuntime`.
  entry .readStaging   as `Prescience.read`, also evaluating conditional and derived givens under an in-macro compiler
      For each field whose instance tier A cannot evaluate, runs the implicit search inside a
      fresh `scala.quoted.staging.Compiler` over the macro classpath (less the current output
      directory) and uses the instance it yields. This sees only givens reachable from global
      scope (companions and package-level givens on the classpath), not lexically scoped ones,
      works only for non-generic class types, and costs a full in-memory compiler run per
      instance, each reported as an info diagnostic with its duration. A field it cannot serve
      takes the runtime tier.

entry .internal   the macro implementation behind `Prescience.read` and `Prescience.readStaging`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. Its public `readStaged` is the macro `Prescience.read` and
      `Prescience.readStaging` splice, with a flag choosing whether tier B runs.
  entry .readStaged   the deriving macro: a reader for a case class, with or without the staging tier
      Not for direct use; called from the splices of `Prescience.read` and `Prescience.readStaging`.

entry .inlinables   sample `Inlinable` givens for `Int`, `String` and `Boolean`, as members of a static object
  detail
      Given aliases defined in `prescience.leaves`, so that they are compiled before any use site
      and tier A can evaluate them through the member-access path (load the object, invoke the
      parameterless accessor). Import them by name, since `import prescience.inlinables.*` does
      not import givens:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import prescience.*
      import prescience.inlinables.{int, string, boolean}

      case class Order(id: Int, name: String, active: Boolean)

      Prescience.read[Order]("7,widget,true")
      ```

      The instances' `Inlinable#readRuntime` methods throw an `AssertionError` by design, so a
      reader which silently fell back to the runtime tier fails loudly: they are demonstrations
      of tier A, not general-purpose instances.
  refer prescience.IntInlinable
  entry .int   `Int is Inlinable`, as `IntInlinable`
  entry .string   `String is Inlinable`, as `StringInlinable`
  entry .boolean   `Boolean is Inlinable`, as `BooleanInlinable`

entry .IntInlinable   a static `Int is Inlinable` which parses the trimmed field text as an integer
  detail
      The generated code is `Integer.parseInt` of the trimmed text. Its `#readRuntime` throws an
      `AssertionError`, since it exists to prove that tier A evaluated it at expansion time.
  entry #Self   `Int`
  entry #read   the code which parses a trimmed `String` expression as an `Int`
  entry #readRuntime   always throws: this instance is meant only to be inlined

entry .StringInlinable   a static `String is Inlinable` whose generated code is the field text itself
  detail
      Its `#readRuntime` throws an `AssertionError`, since it exists to prove that tier A
      evaluated it at expansion time.
  entry #Self   `String`
  entry #read   the field's `String` expression, unchanged
  entry #readRuntime   always throws: this instance is meant only to be inlined

entry .BooleanInlinable   a static `Boolean is Inlinable` which parses the trimmed field text as a Boolean
  detail
      The generated code is `java.lang.Boolean.parseBoolean` of the trimmed text, so anything but
      `true` (in any case) is `false`. Its `#readRuntime` throws an `AssertionError`.
  entry #Self   `Boolean`
  entry #read   the code which parses a trimmed `String` expression as a `Boolean`
  entry #readRuntime   always throws: this instance is meant only to be inlined

entry .Celsius   a sample type whose conditional companion given only the staging tier can evaluate
  detail
      `Celsius(degrees)` has a companion `Inlinable` given with a `DummyImplicit` parameter, so a
      summoned instance is an application rather than a static path, and tier A cannot load it;
      `Prescience.readStaging` resolves and runs it under the in-macro compiler, and inlines its
      code. Its `Inlinable#readRuntime` throws, so a successful read proves the staging tier did
      the work.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import prescience.*
      import prescience.inlinables.string

      case class Reading(celsius: Celsius, station: String)

      Prescience.readStaging[Reading]("21,Kew")   // Reading(Celsius(21), "Kew")
      ```
  entry .inlinable   the conditional `Celsius is Inlinable` given, evaluable only by `Prescience.readStaging`
  entry #degrees   the temperature, in whole degrees Celsius
